[Codex (products & libraries)]: - The current app is not implementing the architecture in your diagram yet. Runtime-wise, it is one Gemini call that returns one JSON result in [`reviewer.py`](C:/Users/PinkPanther/PycharmProjects/Verdict/app/backend/agents/reviewer.py#L28), while [`contracts.py`](C:/Users/PinkPanther/PycharmProjects/Verdict/app/backend/routes/contracts.py#L40) hardcodes all pipeline stages as already done, and [`PipelineTracker.tsx`](C:/Users/PinkPanther/PycharmProjects/Verdict/app/frontend/src/components/PipelineTracker.tsx#L17) is just a timer animation.

- You do already have the start of a future training path:
  - CUAD + LEDGAR download and EDA are present in [`download_datasets.py`](C:/Users/PinkPanther/PycharmProjects/Verdict/scripts/download_datasets.py).
  - That means the repo has a data/eval direction, but not a production inference architecture that matches the diagram.

- One immediate inconsistency: [`app/README.md`](C:/Users/PinkPanther/PycharmProjects/Verdict/app/README.md) says Anthropic setup, but backend code is using `GEMINI_API_KEY`. That should be cleaned up before anything else.

- What is still required to “finalize” the architecture you drew:
  - Real ingestion layer: scanned PDFs, OCR, page structure, tables, clause boundaries, page/span citations. `pdfplumber` is too weak for a serious legal workflow.
  - Real Harvey branch: compare against prior signed contracts / fallback language / precedent repository, not just raw prompt review.
  - Real Kira branch: compare against internal policy/playbook/risk rules in structured form, ideally clause-by-clause.
  - Reviewer ensemble: 3 distinct reviewer roles with different prompts or models, not 3 labels in the UI.
  - Validator logic: explicit agreement thresholds, conflict handling, retry rules, and what gets passed upward.
  - Admin/orchestrator layer: merge Harvey + Kira outcomes, detect disagreement, trigger extra review, produce final recommendation.
  - Evidence layer: every finding needs clause text, location, reason, and source branch that produced it.
  - State + storage: uploaded file, parsed chunks, extracted clauses, reviewer outputs, validator outputs, audit trail, feedback.
  - Async execution: queue/jobs/websocket or SSE updates so frontend reflects real stage status instead of simulated progress.
  - Evaluation harness: golden contracts, expected issues, per-stage precision/recall, hallucination rate, reviewer agreement metrics.
  - Human review loop: approve/reject findings, correct clause labels, feed those corrections into future fine-tuning.
  - Provider abstraction: a model interface so Gemini now and local model later use the same input/output contracts.

- If you want to keep Google API for now, the clean version is:
  - Gemini handles reasoning for reviewer/validator steps.
  - Deterministic code handles orchestration, routing, retries, consensus, policy checks, and citations.
  - All agent outputs use strict schemas.
  - Do not rely on “JSON-looking text” parsing long term. Google supports structured outputs with JSON schema: https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/structured-output

- Best libraries / APIs commonly used for this kind of product:
  - Parsing/OCR:
    - Docling for local, structured document conversion and OCR-friendly pipelines: https://docling-project.github.io/docling/
    - Unstructured for partitioning and ingestion strategies: https://docs.unstructured.io/open-source/core-functionality/partitioning
    - Marker for fast PDF to markdown/JSON conversion: https://github.com/datalab-to/marker
  - Orchestration:
    - PydanticAI for typed agent outputs and graph-style control: https://ai.pydantic.dev/multi-agent-applications/
    - AutoGen for concurrent/multi-agent workflows: https://microsoft.github.io/autogen/stable/user-guide/core-user-guide/core-concepts/agent-and-multi-agent-application.html
    - LangChain/LangGraph if you want graph orchestration and retries, though I would keep it simple unless you truly need distributed agent behavior.
  - Retrieval/storage:
    - Postgres for contracts, outputs, audit data.
    - Vector store only if you need precedent retrieval / policy retrieval / similar-clause search.
  - Model serving later:
    - vLLM or Ollama for local inference.
    - Fine-tuning stack: TRL / Axolotl / LLaMA-Factory depending on the base model you choose.

- Important product point: for the eventual local model, I would not fine-tune “the whole architecture” first.
  - Fine-tune narrow tasks first: clause classification, risk labeling, fallback recommendation ranking, disagreement arbitration.
  - Keep orchestration outside the model.
  - Otherwise you end up with one big opaque model that is hard to debug, hard to trust, and hard to improve.

- Leading products already solving parts of this:
  - Harvey:
    - Strong on workflow builder, legal-specific work, playbooks, Word integration, and institutional knowledge grounding.
    - Good model for your “Harvey + reviewers + workflow” side.
    - Sources: https://www.harvey.ai/platform/workflows , https://www.harvey.ai/platform/word-add-in , https://help.harvey.ai/articles/create-a-playbook
  - Kira / Litera:
    - Strong on high-volume contract review, due diligence, hybrid AI, governance, and review operations.
    - Good reference for your internal policy / clause extraction / diligence workflow side.
    - Sources: https://www.litera.com/ , https://www.litera.com/newslinks/litera-announces-new-kira-experience-empowering-legal-teams-expanded-genai-instant-contract-analysis
  - Luminance:
    - Closest match to your “multi-reviewer + validator consensus” vision because they explicitly market a “Panel of Judges” mixture-of-experts approach.
    - Strong on review, anomaly detection, negotiation, and shared context across contract lifecycle.
    - Sources: https://www.luminance.com/ , https://www.luminance.com/contract-intelligence/
  - Evisort:
    - Strong on extraction, search, OCR, analytics, obligation tracking, and trainable clause recognition.
    - Better reference for repository intelligence than for your reviewer-consensus design.
    - Sources: https://www.evisort.com/product/analyze , https://www.evisort.com/
  - Open-source references:
    - OpenContracts: document analytics + LLM workspace with doc ingestion and extraction concepts you can borrow. https://github.com/JSv4/OpenContracts
    - ContraxSuite / LexNLP: older but still useful for legal extraction/classification patterns. https://contraxsuite.com/contraxsuite/ , https://contraxsuite.com/lexnlp/
    - Accord Project: useful if you move toward machine-readable contracts/playbooks, less relevant for review UX. https://accordproject.org/

- What the leading solutions do well:
  - They ground review in playbooks, precedents, and policy, not only prompt instructions.
  - They show evidence and exceptions, not just summaries.
  - They support Word / redlining / negotiation workflows, which is where legal teams actually live.
  - They have governance, permissions, auditability, and repeatable workflows.

- Their common gaps:
  - Most are expensive and enterprise-heavy.
  - Many are still black-boxy in how recommendations are formed.
  - Local/self-hosted options are much weaker than top SaaS.
  - Very few give you a clean path from “SaaS model today” to “your own fine-tuned local legal agent tomorrow.”

- If this were my product architecture, I’d phase it like this:
  - Phase 1: keep Gemini, replace fake pipeline with real orchestrated stages + strict schemas + evidence spans.
  - Phase 2: add policy/playbook store, precedent retrieval, reviewer disagreement logic, admin merge layer.
  - Phase 3: log human corrections and build eval sets.
  - Phase 4: fine-tune local models for narrow subtasks, then swap Gemini reviewers one by one behind the same interface.
  - Phase 5: only after that, consider a mostly local end-to-end agent.

- The 3 things I need clarified from you before I can map the exact target architecture:
  - Is “Harvey” supposed to check against previous contracts/precedents only, or also external legal standards?
  - Is “Kira” supposed to be only internal policy/playbook validation, or a broader compliance/risk engine too?
  - Do you want the final product to output clause-level redlines and fallback language, or only issue spotting + recommendations?
[Codex (technical patterns)]: - Current state: the backend is a single Gemini call, not a multi-agent graph. `[app/backend/agents/reviewer.py](C:/Users/PinkPanther/PycharmProjects/Verdict/app/backend/agents/reviewer.py#L28)` sends the whole extracted contract to `gemini-2.5-flash` and expects one JSON object back. `[app/backend/routes/contracts.py](C:/Users/PinkPanther/PycharmProjects/Verdict/app/backend/routes/contracts.py#L40)` then returns a hard-coded “Harvey/Kira/Reviewer/Validator/Verdict” pipeline regardless of what actually happened.
- Current state: the frontend pipeline is simulated locally. `[app/frontend/src/components/PipelineTracker.tsx](C:/Users/PinkPanther/PycharmProjects/Verdict/app/frontend/src/components/PipelineTracker.tsx#L17)` advances on timers, and `[app/frontend/src/App.tsx](C:/Users/PinkPanther/PycharmProjects/Verdict/app/frontend/src/App.tsx#L27)` waits for the real API response in parallel. So the UI currently suggests architecture that the backend does not implement.

**Still required to finalize the architecture**
- Add a real orchestration layer. You need an execution graph that explicitly models:
  `ingest -> normalize/chunk -> Harvey branch -> Kira branch -> 3 reviewers per branch -> branch validators -> admin adjudicator -> final recommendation loop`.
- Separate agent responsibilities into distinct prompts/contracts. Right now there is only one reviewer role. You need at minimum:
  `harvey_conflict_detector`, `kira_internal_policy_checker`, `reviewer`, `validator`, `admin`.
- Add shared state between stages. Each stage should read/write a typed state object, not free-form text. Typical fields:
  `document_id`, `policy_id`, `policy_version`, `clauses`, `evidence_spans`, `findings`, `branch_verdicts`, `consensus_score`, `disagreements`, `final_decision`.
- Add policy retrieval/versioning. Harvey cannot check “conflicts with previous policies” unless there is a real source of truth for prior policies, version metadata, and a retrieval/index step.
- Add evidence grounding. Every finding should carry source spans:
  `document page`, `clause text`, `policy section matched`, `confidence`, `rationale`.
  Without this, validator/admin stages become prompt theater.
- Add durable job execution. The current `/upload` request blocks until the LLM returns. For the diagram you want `202 Accepted`, job ID, persisted run state, and streamed progress.
- Add real progress transport. Use SSE or WebSockets so the UI shows actual stage execution instead of timers.
- Add retry/failure policy per stage. Multi-step agent systems fail on parse errors, rate limits, tool failures, and partial outputs. You need stage-level retries, idempotency keys, and resumability.
- Add evaluation harnesses. Before fine-tuning anything, define gold labels for:
  `cross-policy conflict`, `internal inconsistency`, `severity`, `recommendation quality`, `hallucinated citation rate`, `consensus correctness`.
- Add audit storage. Persist prompts, model IDs, inputs, outputs, timings, costs, and review decisions for every stage. This is mandatory if this becomes a legal/compliance product.
- Add auth/multi-tenancy if policies differ by customer. Otherwise “previous policies” and internal-policy checks will leak across tenants.

**Best implementation approaches**
- Lowest-friction path now: keep Gemini, but move to a typed orchestrator in Python with explicit stage functions and Pydantic models. This is the fastest way to make the current diagram real.
- Best medium-term orchestration option: graph/state-machine orchestration.
  LangGraph is a good fit when the workflow is branching, stateful, and agent-heavy.
- Best production-grade reliability option: wrap orchestration in a durable workflow engine.
  Temporal is strong if you need resumability, retries, long-running jobs, and human review steps.
- Best compromise if you want type-safe agent code without going full framework-heavy:
  PydanticAI is a reasonable fit for typed outputs and later durable execution integrations.
- Avoid the common trap of “multiple LLM calls = multi-agent.” If reviewer outputs are not isolated by role, schema, and state transitions, it is just prompt fan-out, not architecture.

**Packages / SDKs / protocols**
- Keep now:
  `google-genai` is fine for the current provider path.
- Add soon:
  `pydantic` models for every stage input/output, not only the final response.
- Strong candidates:
  `langgraph` for graph orchestration.
  `temporalio` if you want durable workflows and resumable runs.
  `fastapi.sse` for browser-native stage streaming.
  `sqlalchemy` + Postgres for run state and audit logs.
  `pgvector` or `qdrant-client` only if “previous policies” becomes semantic retrieval instead of exact version lookup.
- If later moving to local models:
  `vLLM` for serving.
  `llama.cpp` only if you want lightweight local inference, not serious concurrent serving.
  `outlines` or provider-native structured output for schema-constrained decoding.
  `mlx` only if your target runtime is Apple Silicon.

**Google API path: immediate technical fixes**
- Stop parsing free-form JSON from plain text. Google’s structured output docs support JSON schema / typed output directly; use that instead of manual fence stripping plus `json.loads`.
- Add provider abstraction now. Create an interface like `ReviewerModel.generate(stage, input) -> typed_output`. If you hardwire Gemini prompts into business logic, the later local-agent migration will be expensive.
- Replace one giant contract prompt with clause extraction + targeted review passes. The current `text[:30000]` truncation silently drops document content, which will create false negatives on longer policies.
- Add OCR fallback for scanned PDFs. `pdfplumber` only handles extractable text well; scanned PDFs will fail or return partial text.
- Add chunking with overlap and clause segmentation. Legal findings need clause boundaries, not arbitrary character truncation.
- Add per-branch schemas. Harvey and Kira should not emit the same generic structure if they solve different problems.

**Design for the later local-agent / fine-tuned path**
- Do not fine-tune first. First build a dataset from the current pipeline:
  input policy/new policy pairs, expected conflicts, expected internal issues, cited evidence spans, final recommendation.
- Prefer a hybrid path:
  local model for primary reasoning/classification,
  deterministic rules for policy version checks,
  retrieval for prior-policy lookup,
  optional external model fallback for hard cases.
- Fine-tune only after you know which task is unstable:
  clause extraction,
  conflict classification,
  severity scoring,
  recommendation generation,
  consensus/adjudication.
  These are different tasks and may need different training data.
- If the final local agent must replace Gemini fully, keep the orchestration model-agnostic now:
  prompts separate from runtime,
  schema separate from provider,
  tools separate from model,
  persistent state outside the model context window.

**Edge cases / gotchas teams hit**
- “Previous policy conflict” is underspecified unless you define whether conflict means:
  direct contradiction,
  broader/narrower scope,
  version regression,
  duplicate policy,
  exception mismatch,
  approval workflow mismatch.
- Reviewer agreement loops can self-reinforce bad outputs if every reviewer sees the same draft. Better pattern:
  independent first-pass reviews,
  blinded validator,
  admin only sees structured diffs plus evidence.
- Consensus is not accuracy. Three reviewers can agree on the same hallucination if they share prompt bias or the same retrieval context.
- Legal docs often contain tables, appendices, cross-references, and defined terms. Clause-level extraction must preserve section numbers and definitions.
- Long documents will break naive context-window usage. Clause indexing is not optional.
- If policies are tenant-specific, embeddings can cause retrieval of near-duplicate but wrong-tenant policies unless tenant filters are enforced before vector search.
- If you stream stage updates, reconnect/resume matters. SSE supports event IDs and resume semantics; that is useful for long runs.
- FastAPI `BackgroundTasks` is fine for light work, but official docs explicitly caution that heavy jobs should use a bigger worker/workflow solution.

**Community conventions / specs worth following**
- Use typed JSON schemas at every LLM boundary.
- Keep prompts versioned.
- Store evidence spans with every finding.
- Separate orchestration state from UI state.
- Treat model outputs as untrusted until validated.
- Use immutable policy version IDs; never compare against mutable “latest policy” labels only.

**Concrete gap list for this repo**
- No real orchestrator.
- No branch-specific agents.
- No validator/admin implementations.
- No persistence layer.
- No job/run IDs.
- No real-time progress channel.
- No policy repository or version graph.
- No retrieval/index layer.
- No OCR fallback.
- No chunking/clause segmentation.
- No per-stage schemas or structured output enforcement.
- No evaluation dataset.
- No observability/audit trail.
- No abstraction for swapping Gemini with a local model later.

**Recommended next build order**
- 1. Define typed stage schemas and a canonical run-state model.
- 2. Implement policy storage/versioning and retrieval.
- 3. Replace the fake pipeline with a real backend stage runner.
- 4. Stream actual stage events to the frontend with SSE.
- 5. Split the single reviewer into Harvey/Kira/reviewer/validator/admin roles.
- 6. Add persistence, retries, and resumability.
- 7. Build an eval set before any fine-tuning.
- 8. Only then decide whether local inference needs fine-tuning or just better orchestration + retrieval.

- Clarification I need from you: when Harvey checks “previous policies,” is the comparison against all historical versions of the same policy, all policies in the organization, or a curated policy subset?
- Clarification I need from you: should the 3 reviewers be independent parallel reviewers with different prompts/personas, or are they intended to be sequential critique/revision passes?
- Clarification I need from you: for the final local product, do you want one fine-tuned model doing all roles, or multiple smaller role-specific models behind the same orchestrator?

