[Qwen  (architecture + web)]: ## Architecture Findings — Bullet Notes

**Architectural Patterns:**
- Pipeline architecture (sequential: retriever → generator) is most common for RAG
- Need modular design to swap Google API → local LLM later without breaking other components
- Data ingestion pipeline should be separate from query-time pipeline
- Consider adding an orchestration layer between components for better control

**Common Mistakes:**
- Chunking strategy not optimized for PDF structure (tables, headers, multi-column layouts break naive chunking)
- Embedding model choice not validated against domain-specific queries
- No evaluation framework to measure retrieval quality or generation accuracy
- Tight coupling between components makes LLM swap difficult
- Missing feedback loop for continuous improvement (RAGOps concept)

**Security Considerations:**
- Prompt injection risks when PDFs contain malicious content
- PII redaction needed before sending data to external Google API
- Vector database access controls and encryption at rest
- Data poisoning attacks if PDFs are from untrusted sources
- Zero-trust architecture recommended for production
- Guardrails between each pipeline stage

**Performance Considerations:**
- PDF text extraction quality varies significantly by document type (scanned vs. digital)
- Embedding indexing strategy critical for large document sets
- Need caching layer for repeated queries
- Batch processing for ingestion vs. real-time for queries
- Vector DB choice impacts latency (FAISS, Pinecone, Weaviate, etc.)
- Chunk size and overlap parameters need tuning
- Consider hybrid search (keyword + semantic) for better retrieval

**For Local LLM Migration:**
- Abstract the LLM interface behind a common API/protocol
- Plan for fine-tuning data collection from day one (log queries, retrievals, outputs)
- Local LLM will need more infrastructure (GPU, model serving framework like vLLM or Ollama)
